/*
 * Registers the sketches in examples/ for the Doxygen Examples page, with the
 * formatted description shown above each sketch's source. Doxygen does not
 * read comments in the sketches themselves, so keep each block here in step
 * with that sketch's header comment and with the Examples table in README.md.
 */

/**
 * @example EncoderPolling.ino
 * Reads the rotary encoder and its push switch from `loop()`. Prints the count
 * when the knob turns, and toggles the green LED (D1) on each press.
 *
 * The library still counts encoder steps in the background; `loop()` just
 * checks AnalogShield::EncoderCount() and AnalogShield::SwitchPressed()
 * whenever it runs. Compare with EncoderInterrupt.ino, which reacts to the
 * same events with callbacks.
 *
 * @par Jumpers
 * JP3 at 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Count: 4
 * Pressed at count 4
 * @endcode
 * The count changes by 2 per click (AnalogShield::COUNTS_PER_DETENT).
 *
 * @note The switch is not debounced, so a press can occasionally be seen twice
 *       and toggle D1 back off. SerialPlotter.ino shows one way to debounce it.
 */

/**
 * @example EncoderInterrupt.ino
 * Uses callbacks for the rotary encoder and its push switch. The callbacks run
 * inside the interrupt, so they only record what happened; `loop()` does the
 * printing. Prints the count when the knob turns, toggles the green LED (D1)
 * on each press and lights the red LED (D2) while the switch is held.
 *
 * The callbacks are set with AnalogShield::SetEncoderCallback() and
 * AnalogShield::SetSwitchCallback(). Compare with EncoderPolling.ino, which
 * checks the same inputs from `loop()`.
 *
 * @par Jumpers
 * JP3 at 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Count: 4
 * Pressed at count 4
 * @endcode
 * The count changes by 2 per click (AnalogShield::COUNTS_PER_DETENT).
 *
 * @note The switch is not debounced, so a press can occasionally be seen twice
 *       and toggle D1 back off. SerialPlotter.ino shows one way to debounce it.
 */

/**
 * @example Temperature.ino
 * Reads the on-board LMT84 temperature sensor once a second and prints the ADC
 * reading, the signal voltage and the temperature in °C, °F and K, using
 * AnalogShield::TempRaw(), AnalogShield::TempMillivolts(),
 * AnalogShield::TempCelsius(), AnalogShield::TempFahrenheit() and
 * AnalogShield::TempKelvin().
 *
 * @par Jumpers
 * JP1 at U1 (on-board sensor), JP2 at "MCU ADC", JP3 at 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Serial Monitor at 115200 baud, for example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Raw: 1675  1350 mV  22.4 °C  72.3 °F  295.6 K
 * @endcode
 *
 * @par Notes
 * - Change `SOURCE` to TempSource::SENSOR to read the sensor before the
 *   conditioning stage.
 * - TempSource::EXT is meant for an external ADC: on the board's 3.3 V ADC it
 *   only reads above about 21 °C.
 * - Each reading averages `SAMPLES` ADC reads at 12-bit resolution (10 bits on
 *   an Uno R3), which steadies the value. Every column is its own reading, so
 *   they can differ slightly.
 */

/**
 * @example SerialPlotter.ino
 * Streams the encoder, potentiometer and temperature readings to the Arduino
 * IDE Serial Plotter every 200 ms. The encoder switch selects which
 * conditioned temperature channel is read, MCU or EXT.
 *
 * @par Jumpers
 * JP1 at U1 (on-board sensor), JP2 at "MCU ADC", JP3 at 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * Tools → Serial Plotter at 115200 baud, one line per sample:
 *
 * | Label | Value |
 * |---|---|
 * | `Encoder` | Encoder count, 2 per click (AnalogShield::EncoderCount()) |
 * | `Potentiometer` | RV1 wiper voltage, in V |
 * | `Sensor` | Raw LMT84 sensor (`SENSOR_IN`, A1), in °C |
 * | `Temperature` | Conditioned signal on the selected channel, in °C |
 * | `Switch` | Selected channel, -1 for MCU, 1 for EXT |
 *
 * For example:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Encoder:12,Potentiometer:1.650,Sensor:22.41,Temperature:22.37,Switch:-1
 * @endcode
 *
 * @par Channel selection
 * Each press of the encoder switch changes the channel; the switch is
 * debounced in `onSwitch()`. The LED for the selected channel flashes, 500 ms
 * on and 500 ms off:
 *
 * | Channel | Pin | LED | `Switch` |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | MCU | `TEMP_SIG_MCU`, A0 | Green D1 | -1 |
 * | EXT | `TEMP_SIG_EXT`, A3 | Red D2 | 1 |
 *
 * @par Notes
 * - JP2 routes the conditioned signal to only one of A0 and A3, so move it to
 *   "external ADC" when selecting EXT; the other channel reads an unconnected
 *   pin.
 * - EXT is meant for an external ADC: on the board's 3.3 V ADC it only reads
 *   above about 21 °C.
 * - Each reading averages `SAMPLES` ADC reads at 12-bit resolution (10 bits on
 *   an Uno R3), which steadies the value.
 */

/**
 * @example LedFade.ino
 * Fades the green LED (D1) in and out with PWM while the red LED (D2) does the
 * opposite, so one brightens as the other dims. One full cycle, in and out,
 * takes about 2 s.
 *
 * @par Jumpers
 * JP3 at 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * The LEDs only; nothing is printed.
 *
 * @par Notes
 * Both LEDs are active low: the anode goes to VDD and the pin sinks the
 * current, so a lower `analogWrite()` value gives a brighter LED.
 * `setBrightness()` writes 255 - brightness, so 0 is off and 255 is full
 * brightness.
 */
